0.040  Sat Oct  3 15:00:00 2026
    - Fixed a race in the standalone launcher: two simultaneous first runs of
      one binary could each extract the payload into the same cache directory,
      so one run executed while the other overwrote its files (segfaults and
      "failed to extract" in the wild, intermittent failures under parallel
      `prove`). Extraction now happens in a private staging directory that is
      renamed into place atomically; the loser of the rename uses the winner's
      cache. Eight concurrent cold starts of the old launcher failed 14 of 48
      times; the new one 0 of 48.
    - Added `t/standalone_cache_race.t` (in the ATDD gate).

0.039  Sat Oct  3 12:00:00 2026
    - Fixed the application's `share/` tree (`dist_dir()`/`File::ShareDir`
      assets such as the favicon) being absent from the binary: it is now
      bundled at `auto/share/dist/<Dist>/`, so `/favicon.ico` no longer
      answers 500.
    - Fixed saved Perl Ajax routes (`/ajax?file=x.pl`) failing in the binary
      because the process-group launcher ran in a child perl that could not load
      application modules; the launcher is now inlined.
    - Fixed `dashboard stop` dying with "Can't goto subroutine from a sort sub":
      lazy-sub stubs now forward with a call instead of `goto &`.
    - Extended `t/dashboard_service_parity.t` to cover `/favicon.ico` and a
      saved Ajax route; added a sort-comparator regression to
      `t/op_recursion.t`; parity tests extract payloads under their own temp dir.

0.038  Sat Oct  3 09:00:00 2026
    - Added `t/dashboard_service_parity.t`: starts the real web service from the
      interpreter and from the PAX-built binary and compares the HTTP
      conversation (anonymous routes, good/wrong/unknown/empty login, session
      cookie, `/apps`, logout, access after logout), then stops the service even
      when a check fails. It would have caught the `restart` failure and the
      Capture::Tiny load-order bug before they shipped.
    - Verified by differential run against stock Perl (no code change needed):
      installed skills with nested skills, per-level `.env`/`.env.pl` chains
      (`foo.bar.zzz.show`), `.d` hooks, enable/disable/update/uninstall, and the
      layered `.developer-dashboard`/`.d2` command lookup.

0.037  Fri Oct  2 23:30:00 2026
    - Stale hand-written handlers can no longer silently change behaviour. A
      compiled-sub handler is a copy of application code made at one point in
      time; an audit of the d2 build found 95 of 498 handler-compiled subs whose
      real body used methods, arguments or helper calls the handler never
      mentioned. Among them: the password check (constant-time compare, PBKDF2
      path and the unknown-user decoy), the housekeeper (`--dry-run` was ignored
      and would really delete files) and 14 call sites of the clock helper that
      has since required `tz => 'utc'|'local'`. The compiler now keeps a handler
      only when every behaviour token of the real body (methods, helper calls,
      named arguments, hash keys) appears in the handler text or in its record;
      an unreadable body is not trusted either. Otherwise the sub keeps its real
      source, which is always correct. Handlers that exist to relocate packaged
      data carry a `PAX_ADAPTS_PACKAGED_LAYOUT` marker and stay trusted.
    - The clock-helper handlers pass the same `tz` the real source passes,
      derived from the matched body; ambiguous bodies keep the real source.
    - Module-mode units (whole real source evaluated) now put their vouched
      handlers back on top; before, they were ignored in that mode.
    - Added `t/op_drift_guard.t`; `t/dashboard_parity.t` gains the commands that
      exposed these bugs (housekeeper and others).

0.036  Fri Oct  2 20:00:00 2026
    - Fixed three runtime failures in the standalone binary that only showed
      with layered configuration (stock Perl was fine):
      * `.env` files with comments died with "Undefined subroutine
        &PAX::StandaloneRuntime::__SUB__": compiled-sub handlers are now
        compiled with `use feature 'current_sub'`, and the .env comment
        stripper recurses through its own method like the application does.
      * Layered config.json (parent directory + home) died with "Can't use an
        undefined value as a subroutine reference": three recursive handlers
        (config merge, skill-config merge, skill command collection) looked
        themselves up by short name; they now call `$self->$name(...)`.
    - Added `t/op_recursion.t` and layered .env/.env.pl/.d2/config.json/custom
      command scenarios to `t/dashboard_parity.t`, diffed against stock Perl.

0.035  Fri Oct  2 18:00:00 2026
    - A compiled binary is now genuinely portable: it needs only the kernel and
      glibc (libc/libm) on the machine that runs it. Tested in a root file system
      that holds nothing but glibc, the binary and /dev/null: `help`, `init`,
      `doctor`, `ps1`, `jq`, `restart` (HTTP 200) and `stop` all work.
    - Bundled perl now carries libcrypt and libgcc_s (they were assumed to exist
      on the host; recent glibc images no longer ship libcrypt).
    - The runtime module probe no longer subtracts the modules its own preamble
      loaded (strict, warnings, JSON::PP and their dependencies such as
      constant): those were silently left to the host's perl-base.
    - Core pragmas (lib, constant, parent, base, feature, overload, vars, ...)
      are always bundled, because the static scan cannot see lower-case module
      names. Config_heavy.pl, Config_git.pl and the unicore tables, which perl
      loads lazily and which never appear in %INC, are bundled too.
    - `t/blank_container.t` now hides every directory in @INC (including
      perl-base) and the host copies of libcrypt/libz/libssl/libyaml/..., so
      this class of regression fails the test instead of passing by accident.

0.034  Fri Oct  2 12:00:00 2026
    - Fixed a packaging regression from 0.032: the "do not ship the PAX helper
      twice" filter also removed the third-party modules the runtime needs
      (JSON::PP, Digest::MD5, Encode, Storable, Time::HiRes, ...) from the
      bundled payload. On a machine with a full Perl library nothing showed, but
      a lone binary copied to a minimal container failed on every command except
      `version`. Only PAX's own helper modules are filtered now.
    - The runtime defaults DANCER_CONFDIR to the embedded asset directory (unless
      the operator set it), so Dancer2 applications no longer fail with "did not
      pass type constraint ReadableFilePath" when the build-time source path does
      not exist on the machine that runs the binary.
    - Added `t/blank_container.t`: builds a small application and runs the lone
      binary inside a private mount namespace that hides the host's Perl module
      directories (skips without root or unshare), plus a unit test for the
      framework location defaults.
    - Compiler: residual subs that reference a file-scoped lexical declared after
      the first sub (e.g. a lookup hash) now force whole-module residual mode, and
      modules with top-level statements after their subs (e.g. route
      registrations) fall back to packaged source, instead of silently dropping
      them. Fixes `restart`/`stop` and the 404 on every web route.
    - Runtime: residual modules redefine subs in place rather than deleting the
      stash, so call sites bound earlier no longer lose their target.

0.033  Thu Oct  1 12:00:00 2026
    - Reached 100% Devel::Cover coverage (statement, branch, condition and
      subroutine) of lib/ with no `# uncoverable` pragmas, from 48% total.
      Added 36 hermetic `t/cov_*.t` tests (registered in the TDD gate) that
      exercise the compiler recognisers, the standalone runtime, the image
      builder, the CLI, the analysis and dispatch modules, and the small
      planner/guard modules in-process.
    - Code that could not be reached was refactored away instead of being
      excluded: about 440 always-true package-tail operands, duplicate and
      shadowed recognisers, regex terms implied by earlier terms, and
      redundant `// ''`, `defined` and `abs_path(...) || ...` guards. Repeated
      idioms became small tested helpers (`_real_path`, `_slurp_file`,
      `_try_write_bytes`, `_first_executable`, `_quiet_exec_command`).
    - Fixed real bugs found on the way: the `canonical_helper_name` matcher
      regex had an unescaped `||` that made it match any body; the loop-shape
      recognisers returned an undefined induction variable; the masked-mix
      smoke value was 360 instead of 364; `Capture` never detected `local $x`
      or `local @x`; `AppServer::stop` hung up before reading the reply so the
      server died of SIGPIPE; `StandaloneDispatch` never chomped captured
      output; `_compile_launcher` lost its failure reason; the namespace
      rewrite's path form could never match; the extract/inspect children could
      run the caller's code after a failed devnull open; and a bare relative
      output name put the launcher build directory at `/`.
    - `t/standalone_image.t` raises the capture timeout so it also passes under
      the slower Devel::Cover harness.

0.032  Wed Sep 30 22:40:00 2026
    - Cut the standalone build of a real application (Developer Dashboard
      `bin/dashboard`, 74 modules) from about 68s to about 12s. Live
      reference-Perl capture now only runs for units that can contain a native
      loop or arithmetic-leaf shape (`PAX_CODE_UNIT_CAPTURE=always|never`
      overrides), application units compile in parallel (`PAX_JOBS`), and the
      native-artifact analysis gate follows the exact shapes the lowering
      accepts instead of any numeric loop.
    - Made the standalone runtime start faster: op handlers moved out of the
      parsed code into lazily compiled text after `__DATA__`, compiled subs
      install as stubs that build their handler on first call
      (`PAX_EAGER_SUBS=1` disables), heavy imports load on demand, the launcher
      manifest no longer embeds source text or the per-file payload list, and
      CLI router usage text is rendered at build time and replayed. On the
      dashboard CLI `version` went from 88ms to 2ms and other commands run
      about 1.2x to 1.4x faster than stock Perl.
    - Fixed correctness bugs found by comparing the standalone binary with stock
      Perl: file-scoped lexicals were not visible to op handlers, `$/` stayed
      undefined for the whole command after the record read, stored source was
      decoded twice (double-encoded emoji), the CLI router skipped the script's
      own startup statements, sibling data files of bundled modules were
      missing, Moo/Moose declarations were silently dropped from compiled
      units, prototypes were lost on source-compiled subs, and several ops
      called helper subs that did not exist or carried stale copies of the
      application's helper lists, doctor checks, and usage wording. Those ops
      now read the source being compiled or step aside for the real source.
    - Sped up first-run payload extraction in the launcher: the package header
      is parsed without copying the whole payload, files are written with one
      write call each, and directory creation tries the leaf first (about
      17,700 `mkdir` calls down to 1,500).
    - Stopped bundling the PAX runtime helper modules twice (the un-rewritten
      copy from `lib/` duplicated the namespace-aware helper payload).
    - `pax build` now warns when the entrypoint only launches a sibling script
      located through `$Bin` (for example `bin/d2` re-execing `bin/dashboard`),
      since that sibling is not packaged.
    - Dropped code files from the bundled runtime that an earlier inc root already
      carries (they can never be loaded), cutting the dashboard payload from
      2,310 to 1,691 files and 61MB to 51MB; data files stay beside their module.
    - Added `t/build_speed.t` and `t/dashboard_parity.t` (builds a real
      application and diffs the binary's output and exit status against stock
      Perl, and asserts the one-minute build limit). Made the snapshot-only and
      Dancer2-version-specific assertions skip when their inputs are absent.

